PhD Research Proposal
Trustworthy Medical Foundation Models under Acquisition Shift: Calibration, Evidence Grounding and Selective Prediction
Subhan Uddin
Applicant, Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) · School of Computer Science and Engineering, UNSW Sydney
Proposed supervisor: Professor Arcot Sowmya · Proposed co-supervisor: Dr Tahereh Hassanzadeh
Primary imaging modality: Computed Tomography (CT)
Primary clinical task: Pancreatic tumour detection in contrast-enhanced CT (label: tumour‑present vs tumour‑absent)

1. Overview
Medical foundation models are increasingly used as general-purpose encoders for radiological images, on the implicit assumption that large-scale pretraining confers robustness to the variation seen in routine practice. Recent evidence from an adjacent modality questions this assumption: a January 2026 evaluation of fourteen pathology foundation models found that none was invariant to scanner-induced shift, and that robustness did not follow from model size, training scale or recency [1]. Whether the same holds for CT foundation models is not yet established, and is the empirical question this project opens with rather than assumes. The concern is well-motivated for CT on first principles — as set out in Section 2, a CT image is the output of an acquisition process that systematically alters the signal — but it must be demonstrated, not inferred from pathology. The consequences of such failure, where it occurs, are most serious not for accuracy but for reliability: under acquisition and institutional shift, embeddings drift, predicted probabilities become miscalibrated, and a model can be confidently wrong at a hospital it was never validated on — while standard discrimination metrics such as AUC appear stable and conceal the problem.
This project asks how to make medical foundation models trustworthy under acquisition shift, using computed tomography (CT) as the primary modality and pancreatic lesion detection as the primary clinical task. The binary label — tumour-present (n = 1,077) vs tumour-absent (n = 8,824) — is taken directly from the PanTSMini metadata.xlsx (tumor? column, zero missing values). Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma is the dominant tumour type in this dataset, but the label is not restricted to PDAC; the task is pancreatic tumour detection. The early-detection clinical rationale remains: stage at diagnosis governs survival, early lesions are small and low-contrast, and the multi-centre public datasets (PanTSMini, PANORAMA) provide the ground-truth labels across diverse sites. Full DICOM-header shift axes — convolution kernel, tube voltage (kVp) — are not available in either NIfTI dataset; these will be sourced from the Imaging Data Commons (IDC), subject to verification of a task-relevant subset. The project treats calibration, evidence grounding and selective prediction as three facets of one problem: a deployed model should produce reliable probabilities at an unseen site, should ground its outputs in image evidence, and should decline to assert what it cannot support.

