The proposal builds directly on my prior research. My published work addresses detection of small, low-contrast targets under degraded imaging and unpaired-data adaptation across imaging conditions [9,10], and my current research is on source-free test-time adaptation — the exact machinery Objective 3 requires. The move here is from natural-image degradation to clinically defined acquisition shift, and from accuracy to calibrated reliability.
2. Research Problem and Significance
2.1 The problem
A CT scan is not a fixed measurement of anatomy; it is the output of an acquisition process governed by scanner manufacturer, reconstruction (convolution) kernel, slice thickness, tube voltage (kVp), pixel spacing and contrast phase. These parameters vary within a hospital and far more across hospitals, and they systematically alter the image — and therefore the representation a foundation model extracts from it. When the encoder’s representation drifts with acquisition rather than with anatomy or pathology, three failures follow: discrimination may degrade; calibration almost always degrades, so predicted probabilities no longer mean what they claim; and any downstream component consuming the embedding, including a report generator, inherits the error.
The core difficulty is that these failures are hidden by conventional evaluation. A model can retain a strong AUC at a new site while its probability estimates become unreliable, because ranking is preserved even as calibration breaks. Reliability, not ranking, is what clinical use depends on.
2.2 Significance
Clinically, acquisition robustness is a recognised barrier to translation: a model validated at one institution frequently underperforms, or misleads, at another. A system that silently becomes overconfident at a new hospital is more dangerous than one that abstains. For pancreatic cancer specifically, stage at diagnosis is the dominant determinant of survival; even modest gains in reliable early detection on routinely acquired CT carry disproportionate clinical value. Methodologically, the project reframes foundation-model evaluation around calibration and reliability under clinically defined shift, and provides mechanisms — targeted drift reduction, source-free recalibration, selective prediction, evidence-grounded abstention — that make deployment at unseen sites defensible. Because acquisition metadata is recorded in DICOM headers, shift can be defined precisely and reproducibly rather than assumed.
3. Research Gap and Questions
Four gaps in the current literature motivate this project. The research questions that follow address them in turn.
⦁	Evidence of foundation-model fragility under acquisition shift exists for pathology [1] but has not been established for CT, where the acquisition factors and their imaging effects are different. The CT case is assumed rather than tested.
⦁	Robustness work is overwhelmingly evaluated through discrimination metrics such as AUC, which can remain stable while calibration and uncertainty silently degrade. Reliability under shift is under-measured relative to accuracy.
⦁	Drift-reduction and harmonisation methods often reduce shift sensitivity without a guarantee that clinically meaningful signal is preserved. The trade-off between acquisition invariance and diagnostic signal is rarely quantified.
⦁	Source-free recalibration and selective prediction are studied separately and for closed-set classification; they are rarely combined or connected to the reliability of a downstream generation task that consumes the embedding. The deployment-to-application reliability chain is fragmented.
The project therefore asks:
1)	RQ1. How do scanner manufacturer, reconstruction kernel, slice thickness, tube voltage and contrast phase affect the embedding stability, calibration and uncertainty of CT foundation models on the pancreatic lesion detection task, and are these effects visible where discrimination metrics are not?
2)	RQ2. Can acquisition-related representation drift be reduced, using metadata as conditioning signal, while preserving clinically meaningful variation?
3)	RQ3. Can a deployed model be recalibrated at an unseen site without labels (source-free), and can selective prediction reliably identify inputs where it should abstain?
4)	RQ4. Does propagating calibrated uncertainty into report generation allow assertion-level abstention that reduces unsupported findings relative to a standard baseline?
4. Objectives and Methodology
The thesis is organised around four connected objectives. Each addresses one research question and is intended to yield a publishable contribution; each later objective depends on the one before it.
Objective 1 — Characterise embedding stability, calibration and uncertainty under acquisition shift
Three CT foundation models with publicly available weights are selected to span deliberately different pretraining paradigms, enabling comparison of how training objective shapes calibration under shift: CT-FM (ViT-B, self-supervised masked autoencoding on ≈148k volumes — general-purpose 3D CT representation) [2]; CT-CLIP (ViT-based encoder, trained with language supervision on 25k CT–report pairs — vision–language representation) [8]; and VISTA3D (transformer-based interactive segmentation model — included as a segmentation-oriented representation baseline to test whether task-specific pretraining affects calibration stability differently from general-purpose pretraining) [11]. These three are not equivalent or directly comparable models; they are chosen to answer the question of whether pretraining paradigm moderates the acquisition-shift calibration problem, using the pancreatic tumour detection task as the shared evaluation. The selection will be revisited at confirmation of candidature if more suitable alternatives emerge. Shift axes are defined from PanTSMini metadata (manufacturer, CT phase, spacing) and from IDC DICOM headers (convolution kernel, kVp), subject to verification of a task-relevant IDC subset. Methods: embedding-stability analysis under matched-anatomy, varied-acquisition conditions; calibration measured by expected calibration error (ECE) and reliability diagrams; uncertainty quantified and tested for whether it rises appropriately under shift. The clinical task uses the binary tumour? label from PanTSMini metadata.xlsx (tumour-present: n = 1,077; tumour-absent: n = 8,824; 0 missing), with contrast-enhanced scans (n = 5,415) as the primary subset. Deliverable: a reproducible reliability-benchmarking protocol and calibration results across the three models.
Objective 2 — Reduce acquisition-related representation drift while preserving clinical signal
The research question for this objective is: can conditioning a lightweight normalisation layer on available acquisition metadata (manufacturer, CT phase, spacing) reduce representation drift in CT foundation models, while preserving tumour-discriminative information? I will develop acquisition-metadata-conditioned feature normalisation (AMCN): a lightweight conditioning layer inserted between frozen pretrained blocks that takes available acquisition metadata as explicit inputs and produces adaptive normalisation statistics (scale and shift) for intermediate feature maps, analogous to adaptive instance normalisation (AdaIN) but conditioned on clinical metadata rather than a style image. The backbone weights are frozen; only the AMCN parameters are trained. This approach will be compared against standard baselines: z-score normalisation, Nyul histogram normalisation, and no normalisation. An important practical constraint: manufacturer and model are available for only 45% of PanTSMini cases (4,446 and 4,347 of 9,901 respectively). This limits AMCN conditioning to the available subset and means the approach must remain effective when metadata is partially missing — a realistic deployment scenario that will be explicitly evaluated. CT phase (available for 9,900 / 9,901 cases) and spacing (9,901 / 9,901) are used as the primary conditioning signals; manufacturer supplements them where available. Evaluation: ECE and embedding cosine similarity across manufacturer and phase strata, before and after AMCN; AUC on the pancreatic tumour detection task as a guard metric confirming clinical signal is retained; ablation isolating the contribution of each metadata field.
Objective 3 — Source-free recalibration and selective prediction at unseen sites
The research question for this objective is: can a lightweight calibration head, updated online from unlabelled target-site data without access to the backbone or source labels, produce reliable predicted probabilities at an unseen site? I will develop and test entropy-minimised temperature scaling (EMTS) as a hypothesis-driven approach: freeze the pretrained backbone and any AMCN layer entirely; maintain a small site-specific calibration head (a learnable temperature parameter plus a bias term); update it online from unlabelled target-site data by minimising prediction entropy, with exponential moving average smoothing. Three important caveats are built into the evaluation. First, lower entropy does not automatically mean better calibration — a model that becomes overconfident produces low entropy but is poorly calibrated. The evaluation therefore measures ECE directly rather than using entropy as a proxy for calibration quality. Second, the calibration head has minimal parameters, which reduces but does not eliminate the risk of miscalibration under severe shift; if EMTS proves insufficient, this is a result in itself and motivates stronger methods. Third, target-site labels are not used during adaptation; they are held out entirely and used only for final evaluation, ensuring the source-free claim is honest. Selective prediction is implemented as a threshold on post-calibration confidence, estimated from unlabelled data via a held-out entropy bound. Evaluation: ECE and reliability diagrams at unseen PanTSMini sites; risk–coverage curves for selective prediction; stability across sequential sites; comparison against temperature scaling without entropy minimisation as a baseline.
Objective 4 — Evidence-grounded report generation with assertion-level abstention (optional downstream application)
Objective 4 is an optional downstream application contingent on Objectives 1–3 being sufficient for a complete thesis. It is not a separate foundation-model or report-generation research programme; its role is to demonstrate that the calibration and selective-prediction machinery has consequences for a real clinical output. If scope permits, I will investigate how reliability problems from Objectives 1–3 influence CT report generation, and design a generator that abstains from an assertion when the image encoder’s calibrated confidence for the relevant image region falls below the threshold from Objective 3. Using CT-RATE (25,692 CT volumes with paired radiology reports), I will evaluate factual grounding and rate of unsupported findings relative to a standard generation baseline — not surface text-similarity. The thesis retains full value if this objective is de-scoped.
Scope note. The modality-reliance question is included as one analysis within Objective 1 rather than a separate component. The agentic-reasoning direction is excluded. No broad multimodal decision-making claims are made that the selected CT datasets would not directly support. The programme commits to a single primary modality (CT) and a single primary clinical task (pancreatic lesion detection) throughout.
