Dataset access verified. PANORAMA: open on Zenodo and Grand Challenge (~194 GB, NIfTI format); no raw DICOM headers; acquisition parameters limited to NIfTI spacing and matrix dimensions (no convolution kernel, no kVp). PanTSMini: openly available on HuggingFace (BodyMaps/PanTSMini, no login required); metadata.xlsx confirmed to contain manufacturer (GE, Philips, Siemens, Toshiba), manufacturer model, CT phase (arterial/venous/non-contrast), spacing, binary tumour label, and structured radiology reports (~all cases). Convolution kernel and kVp are not available in either dataset; these shift axes are sourced from IDC.
PanTSMini provides the primary clinical benchmark with consistently available CT phase (9,900/9,901), voxel spacing (9,901/9,901), tumour labels (9,901/9,901) and structured reports (9,900/9,901), together with manufacturer and model metadata for approximately 45% of cases. PANORAMA provides a complementary multi-centre European benchmark with binary tumour labels and segmentation masks across contrast-enhanced scans. Together these cover the primary shift axes of CT phase, voxel spacing, site, and — for the available subset — manufacturer. A DICOM-rich dataset (IDC) will be used separately to investigate additional acquisition variables such as convolution kernel and tube voltage (kVp), subject to verification of a task-relevant subset with appropriate labels and confirmed access. AbdomenCT-1K and AMOS serve as cross-task generalisation datasets. CT-RATE and the PanTSMini structured reports are both considered for Objective 4, noting that PanTSMini reports follow a systematic structured format whose suitability for generation research will be assessed against CT-RATE’s clinical prose.
Shift is defined from the following DICOM-derived variables, available in IDC collections (which retain full DICOM headers): scanner manufacturer (0008,0070), convolution kernel (0018,1210), slice thickness (0050,0018), tube voltage kVp (0018,0060), pixel spacing (0028,0030), and contrast phase (from series description or clinical metadata). PANORAMA and PanTSMini provide NIfTI spacing and contrast-phase metadata respectively, serving as partial proxies. Combining IDC-defined shift axes with PANORAMA/PanTSMini clinical benchmarks gives concrete, reproducible experimental variables rather than an abstract notion of ‘domain’. All work with human-derived data will proceed under the relevant data-use agreements and UNSW Human Research Ethics Committee approval.
6. Expected Contributions
⦁	A reproducible reliability-benchmarking protocol for CT foundation models under metadata-defined acquisition shift, centred on ECE and embedding stability rather than accuracy alone.
⦁	Acquisition-metadata-conditioned feature normalisation (AMCN): a lightweight, backbone-frozen method that reduces acquisition-driven drift while demonstrably preserving diagnostic signal.
⦁	Entropy-minimised temperature scaling (EMTS): source-free, backbone-safe online recalibration at unseen sites, with selective prediction for label-free deployment.
⦁	Evidence-grounded report generation with assertion-level abstention (Objective 4, contingent on scope).
7. Indicative Three-Year Plan
⦁	Year 1: Literature review; dataset access and credentialing (PANORAMA, PanTSMini — confirmed open on HuggingFace, IDC, CT-RATE); Objective 1 (reliability benchmarking of three CT foundation models on pancreatic lesion detection); confirmation of candidature; first submission.
⦁	Year 2: Objective 2 (AMCN drift reduction) and Objective 3 (EMTS source-free recalibration and selective prediction); one to two submissions to strong venues (e.g. MICCAI, IPMI, Medical Image Analysis, IEEE TMI); mid-candidature review.
⦁	Year 3: Objective 4 if in scope (evidence-grounded report generation); release of benchmark, code and protocols; thesis writing and final submissions.
8. Risks and Mitigation
⦁	PanTSMini covers the public subset only (9,901 scans) rather than the full 36,390-volume benchmark. The proprietary external sets (UCSF, Polish, Peking University, RSNA) are not publicly available. The public subset spans 145 centres in the metadata and is sufficient for institutional-shift analysis; access to full proprietary sets could be pursued via institutional collaboration in later candidature years if needed.
⦁	AMCN does not reduce shift without suppressing signal. The guard metric in Objective 2 makes this detectable early. If AMCN underperforms, the evaluation contribution of Objective 1 is still complete and publishable.
⦁	EMTS is insufficient for severe site shifts. The risk-coverage evaluation in Objective 3 surfaces this directly; abstention can widen to cover cases the calibration head cannot handle.
⦁	Objective 4 scope. Explicitly optional. Thesis retains full value if de-scoped.
